  "local_bulletin": {
    "source_name": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD)",
    "text": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) — forecast for Kisumu\nValid for 2026-09-20.\nMax 30.0C / Min 21.0C.\nTonight: Partly cloudy\nMorning: Sunny intervals\nAfternoon: Sunny intervals, chance of light showers over few places\nNational outlook mentions this area:\n  - Nandi, Kisumu, Vihiga, Baringo, Bomet, Homa Bay, Nakuru, Bungoma, Busia, Siaya, Migori, Nyamira,",
    "fetched_at_utc": "2026-09-20T03:02:53.873989Z"
  },
  "yesterday_verification_summary": "At Day+0 (2026-09-19), GFS, UKMO, and Kenya Met correctly verified dry conditions, while ECMWF, ICON, and Best Match missed by predicting light precipitation. At Day+3 (2026-09-16), UKMO correctly called a dry day while all other models missed, and at Day+7 (2026-09-12), Best Match verified rain correctly while GFS and ECMWF missed.",
  "narrative_markdown": "## Overview\nSlightly warmer and clearer than yesterday; winds little changed. Dry, with thunder possible from midday, peaking this evening. Temperatures and winds much the same through Wednesday, with , and showers and thunderstorms likely each day.\n\n## Today's Forecast\nDaytime temperatures reaching 32.2°C / 90°F under partly cloudy skies will give way to a low of 18.5°C / 65°F tonight. Dry conditions are expected through the day with 0.0 mm (0.0 in) of accumulation, though scattered convective development brings a 15% probability of a shower or thunderstorm late afternoon into evening. Winds will turn southerly by midday, turning west into the evening, with peak gusts reaching 36.8 km/h (20 kt) from the W. Solar UV index will peak at a very high 9.4 near midday. Air quality ranges from 33 to 150 AQI across ground sensors, with the highest reading at Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central.\n\n## Extended Outlook\nHigher confidence near-term outlook through Wednesday (Sep 23): Moisture and convective instability build across the Nyanza Basin from Monday (Sep 21) through Wednesday (Sep 23), bringing likely daily showers and thunderstorms. Daytime highs on Wednesday will span a model range from 28°C / 82°F to 33°C / 91°F under overcast skies. Lower confidence extended outlook from Thursday (Sep 24) to Sunday (Sep 27): Cloud cover remains high through Friday before clearing toward the weekend. Precipitation chances taper off toward Sunday (Sep 27), when dry conditions return with a 25% rain probability and high temperatures near 33°C / 91°F.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\nConvective instability builds during the afternoon and evening, with peak convective available potential energy (CAPE) reaching 1,900 to 2,490 J/kg across ECMWF, UKMO, and Best Match guidance, peaking around 20:00. While widespread heavy rainfall is not forecast today, thunderstorms remain possible from late afternoon through the evening. Any developing storm carries the hazard of sudden, localized strong wind gusts far exceeding daily baseline forecast winds, presenting a risk to outdoor activities and lake travel.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\nWinds over Winam Gulf will run overnight at 7 km/h (4 kt) gusting 8 km/h (5 kt), then southerly by midday at 5 km/h (3 kt) gusting 16 km/h (8 kt), then into the evening at 10 km/h (5 kt) gusting 19 km/h (10 kt). Peak non-convective gusts are expected around 26.5 km/h (14 kt). Boaters should remain cautious into the late afternoon and night as potential thunderstorm activity over the lake waters can produce sudden, dangerous wind gusts and agitated surface chop.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n### Synoptic Overview\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the northeast (1010 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1019 hPa) — a 9 hPa spread, a moderate large-scale gradient. Pressure is also falling toward the east, north, northwest, south, and southeast — a large-scale feature building in that direction, though this sampling cannot say how fast or whether it will reach here. Pressure overhead is falling (-1.9 hPa 24h trend). Regionally across the Nyanza Basin, mean sea level pressure ranges from 1013.5 hPa at Kisumu to 1016.1 hPa at South Nyanza. Falling pressure trends across the basin over the next 24 to 48 hours support increasing regional moisture influx and convective potential early next week.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\nThe guidance behind this forecast is at least 9 hours old, last aligned at 18:00Z on 2026-09-19. Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) issued a Day+0 forecast calling for a high of 30.0°C / 86°F and low of 21.0°C / 70°F with sunny intervals and a chance of light afternoon showers. Kenya Met's high forecast sits below the numerical model consensus (31.4°C to 33.1°C), aligning closest to UKMO (30.5°C). UKMO systematically under-forecasts overnight lows (+1.4°C bias), whereas ICON systematically over-forecasts overnight lows (-2.5°C bias). Ground air quality sensors report 33 AQI at Kisumu Airport (Good), 44 AQI at Dunga Beach (Good), and 150 AQI at Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central (Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups).",
